{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Orchestrator close verification — every AC re-proven on this host, just now, not asserted from the diff.\n\nThe fix merged as `f58bbe5806df` (\"Bound helpers with a bounder the host actually has\").\n`AGENTS.md` now carries the `bound()` helper with the timeout -> gtimeout -> perl cascade at\n`:286-290`, the `trap 'kill 0'` scope warning, the `pgrep -f` self-match warning, and the\npreserved gate carve-out.\n\n## AC3 — defect-seeded, both halves, run on this macOS host at close time\n\n```\n$ timeout 1 echo hi\n(eval):1: command not found: timeout\nrc=127                                  <- and \"hi\" was NEVER printed\n$ perl -e 'alarm shift; exec @ARGV' 1 echo hi\nhi\nrc=0                                    <- bounds it AND runs it\n```\n\nThe first half is the defect exactly as reported: the bound is absent, the shell reports 127 on\nstderr, and the payload you meant to bound never runs at all. The second half is the corrected\nform doing both jobs.\n\n## AC2 — loud failure when the host has no bounder\n\n```\n$ /bin/sh -c 'PATH=/var/empty; export PATH; if command -v timeout ... || gtimeout ... || perl ...\n             then echo UNEXPECTED; else echo \"no wall-clock bounder (timeout/gtimeout/perl): not spawning\" >&2; exit 127; fi'\nno wall-clock bounder (timeout/gtimeout/perl): not spawning\nrc=127\n```\n\nWorth recording that my FIRST attempt at this proof was invalid: I ran `env PATH=/var/empty sh -c`,\nwhich failed to find `sh` itself and returned 127 for the wrong reason — a false pass that looked\nidentical to a real one. Re-run with the shell resolved by absolute path so only the BOUNDER\nlookup is emptied. Noting it because \"127 for the wrong reason\" is the precise failure mode this\nwhole ticket is about, and it nearly slipped into its own closing evidence.\n\n## ACs verified by inspection of merged `AGENTS.md`\n\n- AC1 cascade selects whichever bounder the host has — `:286-290`.\n- AC4 `trap 'kill 0'` names its scope: \"signals the caller's *process group*, which is both too\n  wide and too narrow: a subshell shares its parent's group, so it kills your own script ... while\n  a wrapper shell above you is never in the group and survives\".\n- AC5 `pgrep -f` warning present: \"matches command-line TEXT, not identity\", with the corrected\n  form (wait on the recorded pid; exclude `$$`; confirm with `ps`).\n- AC6 gate carve-out preserved: still says not to bound `review-plan`, `verify-completion`, a\n  gated close, or `make verify`.\n- AC7 portable — every check above is a plain shell command, no CI provider, no new dependency.\n- AC8 `make lint` / `make typecheck` green in CI on the merge.\n\n## Close class\n\n`env_integration`: the guidance was correct against a GNU-coreutils host and silently wrong on\nthis macOS one, where `timeout` does not exist. The defect is the platform gap, not a regression\nin behaviour.\n\n## Sibling still open\n\n`6a9d-4792` (`publicized-chuffy-guanaco`) — `docs/orphaned-processes.md` still prescribes the\nbare `timeout` and `trap 'kill 0'` pattern this ticket removed from AGENTS.md, and AGENTS.md now\nlinks to that document for incident evidence. It was deliberately kept separate because widening\nthis ticket's `file_impact` mid-flight would have invalidated its signed plan-review attestation.\nClosing this one does NOT close that; the defective prescription is still reachable in two clicks."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788573940655561001,"uuid":"dee0af4e-a1c6-4a9a-998d-88f4b39ab903"}